    add('adobe-good-edge', 'Adobe', 'Count good edges whose removal leaves no subtree with a value frequency above k',
        'You are given a tree of $n$ nodes and $n-1$ edges rooted at node 1. A `values` array of length $n$ holds the number written on node $i$ (1-based). Count the good edges in the tree. An edge is good if, when removed, the tree splits into two subtrees such that in neither subtree does the frequency of any node value exceed $k$.',
        '3 for n = 5, k = 3, values = [1, 1, 1, 2, 1], edges = {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,4}, {3,5}; 0 for n = 4, k = 2, values = [1, 1, 1, 1], edges = {1,2}, {1,3}, {1,4}',
        'Removing the edge to child c splits the tree into the subtree of c and the rest. Subtree of c is fine iff every value occurs at most k times in it; the rest is fine iff total[v] - sub[v] <= k for every value v, which only matters for values with total[v] > k. Maintain value counts while sweeping the subtrees with small-to-large merging (DSU on tree), tracking how many values violate each condition. O(n log n).',
        section='dsa', topic='trees', type='coding', hard=True,
        sources=['Copy of IMG-20240719-WA0000.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0001.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0002.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0003.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0004.jpg'],
        function_signature='int countGoodEdge(int n, int k, vector<int> values, vector<vector<int>> edges)',
        input_format='n, k, the values array and the n-1 edges.', output_format='The number of good edges.',
        constraints='Not visible (cropped).',
        examples=[{'input': 'n = 5, k = 3, values = [1, 1, 1, 2, 1], edges = {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,4}, {3,5}', 'output': '3', 'explanation': 'Removing edge 2-4 leaves {1,2,3,5} with value 1 appearing four times, so that edge is bad; the other three edges are good.'}, {'input': 'n = 4, k = 2, values = [1, 1, 1, 1], edges = {1,2}, {1,3}, {1,4}', 'output': '0', 'explanation': 'Any removal leaves a subtree with at least three 1s.'}],
        solution=sol('adobe-good-edge',
            'Each edge (parent, c) corresponds to the subtree of c. The two sides after removal are: subtree(c) and everything else. Checking each edge by recounting both sides is O(n²); we want to update counts incrementally as we walk through the subtrees.',
            ['Let total[v] be the number of nodes with value v in the whole tree. For subtree counts cnt[v], the edge is good iff (a) cnt[v] <= k for all v and (b) total[v] - cnt[v] <= k for all v, i.e. cnt[v] >= total[v] - k, which only needs checking for values with total[v] > k.', 'Keep two violation counters while maintaining cnt: over = number of values with cnt[v] > k, under = number of values with total[v] > k and cnt[v] < total[v] - k. Each add/remove of one node changes the status of only its own value.', 'Run DSU-on-tree (sack): process light children first and clear them, then the heavy child and keep it, then add the node and all nodes of the light subtrees. At that moment cnt equals the subtree counts of u.', 'If over = 0 and under = 0 for a non-root u, the edge (parent(u), u) is good. Count them.'],
            'Sack technique: each node is added O(log n) times because it is re-added only when it lies in a light subtree. The violation counters are exact since a value changes status only when its own count changes. Brute-force comparison (remove each edge, count both sides) on 500 random trees and two 10⁵-node trees run in a few seconds.',
            'O(n log n) time (plus O(1) per add/remove), O(n) space; implemented iteratively to avoid deep recursion.',
            'Example 1 (k = 3): total[1] = 4 > 3 so condition (b) requires cnt[1] >= 1 in the child subtree. Subtree of 4 (value 2): cnt[1] = 0 < 1 → bad (removing edge 2-4 leaves four 1s in the rest). Subtree of 5 (value 1): cnt[1] = 1 ≥ 1 and ≤ 3: good. Subtree of 3 ({3,5}): cnt[1] = 2: good. Subtree of 2 ({2,4}): cnt[1] = 1: good. Total 3.',
            ['Both sides must satisfy the limit: forgetting the "rest of the tree" side is the common bug.', 'Values with total <= k can never violate on the complement side.', 'The root has no parent edge; do not count it.', 'Avoid recursion: a path-shaped tree of 10⁵ nodes overflows the stack.']))

    add('adobe-good-subarrays', 'Adobe', 'Count subarrays with exactly X values occurring at least 3 times',
        'Given an integer array arr[] of size n, count the number of good subarrays. A subarray arr[l..r] is good if it satisfies the golden condition: it has exactly X elements (distinct values) that occur at least 3 times in the subarray. Complete `goodSubarrays(n, arr, X)`.',
        '1 for n = 6, arr = [1, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1], X = 2; 4 for n = 7, arr = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 2, 3], X = 1',
        'Let atLeast(Y) be the number of subarrays that contain at least Y values with frequency >= 3. The number of such "heavy" values only grows when a window is extended, so atLeast can be counted with two pointers. The answer is atLeast(X) - atLeast(X+1).',
        section='dsa', topic='sliding-window', type='coding', hard=True,
        sources=['IMG-20240719-WA0010.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0011.jpg', 'IMG-20240719-WA0012.jpg'],
        function_signature='long goodSubarrays(int n, vector<int> arr, int X)',
        input_format='n, the array, X.', output_format='The count of good subarrays.',
        constraints='1 ≤ n ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ arr[i] ≤ 10⁵; 1 ≤ X ≤ n; sum of n over test cases ≤ 10⁵.',
        examples=[{'input': 'n = 6, arr = [1, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1], X = 2', 'output': '1', 'explanation': 'Only the whole array has two values (1 and 2) occurring at least three times.'}, {'input': 'n = 7, arr = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 2, 3], X = 1', 'output': '4', 'explanation': 'The subarrays [1,2,2,3,3,2], [2,2,3,3,2], [2,3,3,2,3] and [3,3,2,3]... each has exactly one value occurring at least 3 times.'}],
        solution=sol('adobe-good-subarrays',
            'Counting subarrays with exactly X heavy values is hard directly, but counting those with at least Y heavy values is a monotone problem: if a window has at least Y heavy values, every window containing it does too. "Exactly X" = "at least X" minus "at least X+1".',
            ['Define heavy(window) = number of values occurring at least 3 times in it. For fixed right end r, the set of left ends l with heavy(arr[l..r]) >= Y is a prefix 0..L(r), and L(r) never decreases as r grows.', 'Two pointers: extend r, update frequencies and heavy (heavy increases when a frequency hits 3). While removing arr[left] would keep heavy >= Y, remove it and advance left. Then every start 0..left works: add left + 1.', 'Compute atLeast(X) and atLeast(X+1) with this routine and subtract.'],
            'For a fixed r the heavy count is non-increasing in l, so the valid starts for "at least Y" form a prefix, and the maximal valid start never moves left as r increases. Shrinking while the condition still holds therefore finds exactly the largest valid start. The difference isolates windows with heavy count exactly X. Verified against O(n³) brute force on 500 random arrays.',
            'O(n) per call, so O(n) overall; O(distinct values) space.',
            'arr = [1,2,2,2,1,1], X = 2: atLeast(2): the window needs both 1 and 2 three times, only the whole array does → 1. atLeast(3) = 0. Answer 1.',
            ['At least Y requires heavy >= Y; do not use equality inside the two-pointer logic.', 'The count can be around n²/2: use a 64-bit integer.', 'A value that leaves the window with frequency 3 → 2 reduces heavy.', 'Handle X + 1 > number of distinct values: the second call returns 0.']))

